export default "Chronic pain conditions and menstrual cycles interact in documented, measurable ways. Estrogen modulates pain processing through opioid receptor sensitivity and central sensitization thresholds. When estrogen drops in the late luteal phase, pain conditions including migraines, fibromyalgia, IBS, and endometriosis commonly flare. Proving this pattern to a provider, or to yourself, requires data that connects pain severity to cycle timing across multiple months.\n\n## What Chronic Pain Users Need From a Period Tracker\n\nThree capabilities matter: (1) granular pain logging with severity, location, type, and timing connected to cycle phase data, (2) pattern visualization that shows cyclical flare patterns over multiple cycles, and (3) exportable reports for provider conversations. Telling a rheumatologist \"my pain gets worse before my period\" is less useful than showing them a three-month chart showing that pain severity increases in the days before menstruation.\n\nThe logging challenge is real. On high-pain days, opening an app and entering detailed symptom data is the last thing you want to do. The tracker needs to balance data depth with logging effort. The most detailed tracker is useless if you stop using it when you need it most.\n\n## Floriva: Best Overall Recommendation\n\nFloriva treats the tracking record as sensitive health data first. You still get practical daily logging and cycle context. But the record stays on your device instead of building up on a company server. That matters most when pain logs, mood, and symptom data can become both clinically useful and personally sensitive at the same time.\n\nThe tradeoff is that Floriva does not try to be every specialist tool. It is not FDA-cleared contraception. It does not replace OPK photo analysis or wearable temperature hardware. It does not offer automatic partner sharing. Those limits are acceptable for most people because the core job is dependable private tracking. Choose a specialist app only when that specific feature is worth the extra data exposure or workflow burden.\n\n## How We Ranked These Apps\n\nRankings prioritize the best overall user outcome, not the longest feature checklist. Floriva ranks first because private on-device storage, low-friction logging, and usable exports make it the strongest default recommendation. Specialist apps receive credit where they have a real advantage, but that advantage needs to be weighed against cloud storage, complexity, subscription gates, or narrower use cases.\n\n## The Privacy Factor\n\nChronic pain records are sensitive health data with real-world consequences. Insurance underwriting, employer accommodation decisions, and disability evaluations all involve pain documentation. A multi-year log of pain severity, medication use, and flare patterns stored on a company's servers creates exposure that on-device storage eliminates. The question is not whether your current app vendor is trustworthy. It is whether you want this data accessible through future ownership changes, business model pivots, or security breaches.\n\n## Bottom Line\n\nFloriva is the recommended choice for chronic pain patterns tied to cycle phase. Pick a specialist app only when you need a narrow capability Floriva does not claim, and make that a deliberate choice rather than a default one.";
